on.\n\nLet's evaluate the possible plays:\n\n- Play W1: Discard W1. Then, the hand has T9x2, F1, F3, F4, J1, J3. If they can draw a W1, they can form a Pung of W1 (but they have only one in hand, so need two more). Wait, the unshown tiles have W1:3. So if they discard W1, they can draw a W1 to form a Pung. But forming a Pung requires three of the same tile. The user would need to have two in hand and draw the third. But if they discard W1, they have none left. Unless they draw a W1, but then they would have one in hand and one drawn. No, that's not enough. Wait, maybe the user is thinking of a different approach. Alternatively, maybe the user can form a Chow with W1, but there's no way to form a Chow with W1 unless they have W2 and W3. But the user has W3 in the meld (W345). So no, that's not possible.\n\nWait, the user's melds include a W345 Chow. So they have a W3 in the meld. If the user has a W1 in hand, maybe they can form a W123 Chow if they get a W2 and W3. But the user has W3 in the meld, but that's already used in the existing Chow. So the user can't use it again. So that's not possible.\n\nSo the only way to form a meld with the remaining tiles is to form a Pung of an honor tile. The user has F1, F3, F4, J1, J3. If they discard one of these, they can wait for another to form a Pung. For example, if they discard F3, they can wait for another F3. The unshown tiles have F3:2. So there are two F3s left. If the user discards F3, they can draw an F3 to form a Pung. Similarly for F1, F4, J1, J3. The unshown counts for these are:\n\n- F1:1\n- F3:2\n- F4:3\n- J1:3\n- J3:1\n\nSo if the user discards F3, there are two left. Discarding F1 leaves one left. Discarding F4 leaves three. Discarding J1 leaves three. Discarding J3 leaves one. So the best options are F4 or J1, since they have more tiles left. But forming a Pung of F4 would require three F4s. The user has one in hand. If they discard F4, they can draw two more. The unshown has three F4s. So if they discard F4, they can draw two F4s to form a Pung. Similarly for J1, unshown has three J1s. If the user discards J1, they can draw two more to form a Pung.\n\nBut the user's current hand has a pair of T9s. If they can form a Pung of an honor tile, then they would have four melds (two Chows, one Pung) and a pair. That would be a valid hand. The points for that would depend on the patterns. For example, if they form a Pung of J1, that would add a Dragon Pung (worth 2 points). The existing hand has two Chows, which might contribute to All Chows (4 points). The pair of T9s would contribute to Outside Hand (4 points if all melds and pair are terminals or honors). Let's calculate:\n\n- Two Chows (W345 and B789): All Chows (4 points)\n- Pair of T9s: Outside Hand (4 points)\n- Pung of J1: Dragon Pung (2 points)\n- Total: 4 + 4 + 2 = 10 points. But the minimum to win is 8 points, so that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, if they form a Pung of F4 (Wind tile), that would be a Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point). All Chows (4 points). Outside Hand (4 points). Total 9 points. Also sufficient.\n\nSo the best options are to discard a tile that allows forming a Pung of an honor tile with more remaining tiles. Between F4 and J1, J1 has three left, F4 has three. So either is good. But the user has J1 in hand. If they discard J1, they can draw two more to form a Pung. Similarly for F4.\n\nBut the valid actions include Play F4 and Play J1. So the user can choose to discard either. However, the user's hand also has F1, which is only one left. Discarding F1 would make it harder to form a Pung.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user can form a Seven Pairs if they discard a tile and then draw a specific one. For example, if they have a pair of T9s, and if they can form other pairs. But with the current tiles, it's not possible. So the best option is to discard a tile to wait for a Pung of an honor tile.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the user can play F3, F1, F4, J1, J3, W1, or T9. The best options are to play